      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e The two cases are a bit different in my view.\n\u003e \u003e \n\u003e \u003e The target branch may have been deleted while the change still exists\n\u003e \n\u003e Yes, but this is the indexing event of a change: if the change has been created or updated, the branch *must* exist. Any reindexing would also fail if the target branch doesn\u0027t exist.\n\nYou\u0027re right that Gerrit does not allow deleting a target branch while it has open changes.\n\nHowever, you can still abandon the change targeting a deleted branch. Also, if changes targeting a deleted branch have been merged, they will still exist.\n\nAfter the branch deletion, these changes can still be reindexed without any issues.\nYou can add a post-merge -1, for example, or a comment.\n\nI tested this, and changes targeting the deleted branch are handled correctly at the moment in multi-site and reindexed as expected.\n\n\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e The meta ref is different because a change just cannot exist without it; that is an error, and returning `null` would hide an inconsistent state.\n\u003e \n\u003e No, the change could have been deleted, and therefore, the `/meta` could be absent, which is a valid use case.\n\nThis is different.\nDeleted changes take a different path: `onChangeDeleted()` creates a `deleted\u003dtrue` event directly and does not call `getMetaSha()`.",
